title op6: a lost name held by a directory cannot supply a file's dnode

# VR7, and the symmetric twin of op6-claimed-guard.  The kernel lane
# named two guards that the lost-set fallback needs exactly as much
# as the primary name-driven path does.  The claimed guard now has an
# executable witness; this is the other one.
#
# Base holds one file under two names, x and y, beside an empty
# directory d.
#
# ONTO frees the name x and gives it to the directory: it unlinks x
# from the file, and renames d to x.  The file keeps y and keeps its
# dnode; the directory keeps its dnode too.  Nothing is destroyed --
# this is an ordinary reorganisation, and the name x simply changes
# hands.
#
# OFF-OF renames the file wholesale: x becomes aa and y becomes bb.
# Its delta is lost {x, y}, gained {aa, bb}, and no gained name
# exists anywhere in onto.
#
# So realization for the output file pool falls to pass two, and the
# lost-set route asks where {x, y} live in onto.  The answer is two
# different places:
#
#   y -> pool 10-100, the file.  Type matches.
#   x -> pool 20-100, a DIRECTORY.  Type does not.
#
# WITH THE TYPE FILTER the second is struck out before the route is
# read, one fragment remains, and the file keeps dnode 10-100.
# Clean, and correct: the user renamed two links and moved a folder,
# and neither of those is an event.
#
# WITHOUT IT the pool appears to straddle two holders, and the two
# ways that goes are both wrong.  Report a straddle and the fixture
# conflicts over nothing -- a spurious conflict of exactly the kind
# Section 16 was written to delete.  Take the directory's dnode and a
# file pool is realized onto a directory, which is worse than a
# conflict because an applier will try it.
#
# The guard is not symmetry for its own sake.  A lost name is a name
# in BASE, and the thing standing at that name in onto need have
# nothing to do with the pool that used to hold it.  That is exactly
# what makes the fallback riskier than the name-driven path it backs
# up: a decided name is a name the output pool actually has, while a
# lost name is only a name it used to have.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none.  /shared is green; d and x are the same
#                    directory pool, corresponded by red
#   deltas:          the file pool lost {x, y} and gained {aa, bb}.
#                    The directory pool lost {d} and gained {x} on
#                    the onto face
#   decisions:       the file pool reuses onto's dnode 10-100 by the
#                    lost-set route, after the type filter strikes
#                    out x's directory holder.  The directory keeps
#                    dnode 20-100 under its new name x
#   conflicts:       none.  Off-of vacated x and onto occupied it, so
#                    the two changes compose rather than collide
#   emission:        two renames on the file inside /shared's scope;
#                    the directory's rename is onto's and is already
#                    in the clone, so it emits nothing

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y
      20-100: d/

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: y = v0
      20-100: x/

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb
      20-100: d/

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: aa = v0
      10-100: bb
      20-100: x/
